input-funbox) "rules" "RULES?" nil) (om-load-inputfun (quote input-funbox) "rules" "RULES?" nil))) (om-make-point 217 457) nil nil nil nil 1))) (when (fboundp (quote set-active)) (set-active box nil)) box) (let ((box (om-load-boxcomment "metric-accents constrains rhythmic accents in relationnto the underlying meter (here: the mixed rhythm domainn((1/16) (1/8) (1/4) (1/2)) connected to the engine).nEvaluate the engine box, then the score.nPlay with strictness, accent rule, rule type.nSee sources/rhythm-rules.lisp, metric-accents." (om-make-point 397 465) (quote "The rule metric-accents controls the metric position of $accented notes. $$What counts as an accent can be set with the argument $accent-rule. Some accent rule cases can be selected simply $with the menu of that argument. For example, :longer-than-$predecessor specifies that any note that is longer than its $preceding note and not shorter than the following note is $considered an accented note. $$The argument strictness controls how such accents are $constrained. When set to :note, any accented note occurs $on a metric position specified by the argument metric $structure (either the first beat of a bar or any beat). $However, also unaccented notes can occur on such metric $positions. When the strictness is set to :position, then every $metric position specified by the argument metric structure $will carry an accented note. However, such accents can also $occur between such metric positions.  $$There does not exist general agreement on what defines an $accent. Therefore, accent rules can also be given as argument $instead of using the menu, and such accent rules can even $support arguments themselves. The box $mk-accent-has-at-least-duration-ar returns a rather simple $accent rule: any note beyond the specified duration is $considered an accent. $$BUG: in metric-accents: the first and last two notes may $not follow this rule.") "" (om-make-point 613 299) nil (om-make-color 0 0 0) (om-make-font "Verdana" 12 :fa